<Lesson slug="explore-the-details">
  <section class="reading">
    <h2>What goes under a task</h2>
    <p>The space beneath the backbone and the tasks is the <dfn>body</dfn> of the map. Patton names four kinds of card that belong there.</p>
    <dl class="terms">
      <div><dt>Details</dt><dd>Smaller steps or specifics inside the task. <em>The notice says which item types this session takes.</em></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Alternatives</dt><dd>Another way of doing the same thing. <em>Book by phone, or book on a form.</em></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Variations</dt><dd>What a different person would do, or the same person on a different day. <em>A visitor turns up without a booking.</em></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Exceptions</dt><dd>What happens when something goes wrong. <em>The session is full. The lamp cannot be fixed.</em></dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>You do not need to label cards with these words. Treat them as four questions to ask at every column.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="reading">
    <h2>Play What-About</h2>
    <p>Patton credits his friend David Hussman with the name for this. Stand at a task and ask “what about…?” What about someone with no phone? What about a day when the session is full? Each answer becomes a card beneath that task.</p>
  </section>

  <WhatAbout />

  <section class="reading">
    <h2>The map always grows</h2>
    <p>After one round of What-About, the café map has twenty-one cards in its body, and the team could keep going. Patton has a line for the unease this causes.</p>
    <blockquote class="pull"><p>Scope doesn’t creep; understanding grows.</p></blockquote>
    <p>The work was always there. The map shows it to you before you start building, when it is cheapest to find. Nothing on the map is a commitment, either. Exploring is for seeing the options, and lesson 6 is where you choose between them.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="reading">
    <h2>Now maps and later maps</h2>
    <p>The café map describes a visit as the team hopes it will go once something has been built. Patton calls that a <dfn>later map</dfn>. You can also map how things work today, which is a <dfn>now map</dfn>. On a now map you mark the pains, such as Sam’s forty-minute queue, and the joys, such as fixing the lamp alongside the repairer. You also write down your questions.</p>
    <p>When a team does not yet understand the problem well, the now map is the place to start. It shows where the later map has to be different and which parts to leave alone.</p>
    <p>Exploring brings other people onto the map as well. Someone has to tell visitors who is repairing on Saturday. That is Noor’s task, and it happens before Sam’s visit begins. The rota editor that lesson 3 set aside belongs there, in Noor’s part of the story. Give each kind of user their own cards, at the point in the story where they act.</p>
  </section>
</Lesson>
